---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Senior Product Designer for B2B SaaS fraud and risk. Formerly at Kount, an Equifax company, where I designed the rules engine, review console, and design system."
>
  <header class="hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="display hero__title">
      I design the surfaces people use when a model, a policy, and a person have to agree.
    </h1>
    <p class="display hero__outcome">
      Fraud, payments, and risk products for B2B SaaS. Ten years in product design, five of them at Kount, an Equifax company, designing for a fraud platform that handled 1M+ transactions a day.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose shell">
    <h2>The work</h2>
    <p>
      Risk products fail in a specific way. The model can be right and the reviewer still cannot defend the decision. The policy can be sound and still unpublished because the editor is a production risk. The network can set a floor and the bank reviewing the payment still cannot tell whose rule fired.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the work I want. At Kount I designed the rules engine, the review console, and a design system used by 1 team on a platform with 7+ products. I shipped design on Kount 360, Payments Fraud, Authorized Payment Protection, Account Protection, and Dispute and Chargeback Manager, including the original information architecture for Policy Management. I sit with data science on what a score is allowed to reveal, and with engineering on what a configuration surface is allowed to publish into live traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also ship the interface in code when that proves the point faster. I rebuilt a slice of the trust score review console as a <a href="https://mastaobass.github.io/trust-score-ds/console/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">public React app</a>, and I prototype faster with Figma Make and Cursor.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this work</h2>
    <p>
      I came to design sideways. Four and a half years in a biochemistry lab, cognitive science with an HCI focus at UC San Diego, then a Master of Engineering in Product Design and Development Management at Northwestern.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lab is the part that still shows up. Bench science teaches you that the hard part of a problem is usually the measurement, not the answer, and that a result you cannot explain is not a result yet. Fraud tooling is the same job in a different building.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      In these products the interface is downstream of a model, a policy, and a regulator. None of those are negotiable from Figma. I start with what the system actually does: what the model can reveal, what the policy allows, and what a reviewer is permitted to change. Then I design the smallest surface that makes origin, authority, and next action legible.
    </p>
    <p>
      I raise structural risk early and I do my own design QA. On the rules engine, QA surfaced where dropped features left incomplete experiences, like a V1 locking mechanism that made it unclear who had access to change things.
    </p>
    <p>
      I speak Spanish. On authorized payment protection that put me in the stakeholder room with a Latin American payment network instead of reading the meeting afterward.
    </p>

    <h2>Now</h2>
    <p>
      Seattle, working remotely. Open to senior and staff product design roles, and to hybrid design-engineer seats, on risk, payments, and trust products. This site is Astro. The component kit is React, TypeScript, and Storybook.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Match CaseStudy hero rhythm and deck typography. */
  .hero { padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-lg); }
  .hero__title { font-size: var(--type-h1); margin-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-lg); }
  .hero__outcome {
    font-size: var(--type-h3);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  /* Page ends after prose (no MetricGrid / NextCase). */
  .prose { padding-block-end: var(--space-3xl); }
</style>
